WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.552
Welcome. This is the Risk
Mechanism Theory Index, RMTI,

00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:09.693
created and formulated by
Dr. Cesar Marolla to quantify

00:00:09.693 --> 00:00:13.039
systemic risk in disaster
science and in oncology.

00:00:13.438 --> 00:00:17.435
In the next few minutes you will
see how one equation turns a hazard,

00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:22.175
the people exposed, how fragile they
are, and how well a system copes,

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:26.568
into a single bounded score.
A reminder before we start.

00:00:26.885 --> 00:00:32.901
RMTI is a research prototype: not
validated, not a medical device,

00:00:33.005 --> 00:00:36.350
and not for patient-care
or emergency decisions.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.562
Everything here uses illustrative values,
published rows, or aggregate counts.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.492
No patient data appears. Why an index?

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.669
Risk decisions are often made from
narrative: the loudest voice,

00:00:49.669 --> 00:00:55.153
or the most recent event. RMTI
moves from narrative to algorithm.

00:00:55.457 --> 00:01:00.898
Four inputs, each between zero and one,
each with a stated meaning and source.

00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:06.032
And the same fixed thresholds for every
patient, every city and every year,

00:01:06.275 --> 00:01:09.962
so a score can be compared,
audited and challenged.

00:01:10.544 --> 00:01:16.748
The equation multiplies four bounded factors:
likelihood, exposure, vulnerability,

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.245
and resilience, which
enters as one minus rho.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.608
L is likelihood: how probable
the hazard is in a year.

00:01:24.919 --> 00:01:29.857
In oncology it comes from a validated risk
score, such as aMAP for liver cancer,

00:01:29.981 --> 00:01:31.555
and is not re-derived.

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:35.620
In disaster science it is the
annual probability that a flood,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.576
fire or heat event materially strikes.

00:01:39.044 --> 00:01:42.902
E is exposure: who or
what is in harm’s way.

00:01:43.251 --> 00:01:45.474
For liver surveillance it is the share of

00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:48.655
the at-risk population inside
the programme’s footprint.

00:01:48.915 --> 00:01:53.244
For a city it is the share of people
and assets inside the hazard zone.

00:01:53.737 --> 00:01:58.009
V is vulnerability: how
badly those exposed are hurt.

00:01:58.337 --> 00:02:01.849
For the liver it is reserve,
taken from the ALBI grade,

00:02:01.978 --> 00:02:07.232
which uses albumin and bilirubin. For
a city it reflects housing, services,

00:02:07.232 --> 00:02:10.811
poverty and governance. Multiply L,

00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:16.200
E and V and you get the inherent risk:
the score if nothing cushioned it.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:21.623
Resilience, rho, is the fraction of
that inherent risk the system absorbs.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:27.818
Because it enters as one minus rho, more
resilience always means less residual risk.

00:02:28.135 --> 00:02:32.257
And that is where action lives.
Improve surveillance quality,

00:02:32.257 --> 00:02:38.162
early warning or infrastructure, raise
rho, and the residual risk R falls.

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:43.678
R lands in one of five fixed
tiers: low, moderate, high,

00:02:43.771 --> 00:02:47.681
severe and critical. Each
tier carries a posture.

00:02:48.031 --> 00:02:49.221
For liver surveillance,

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:55.277
it runs from usual-care monitoring
at tier one to an immediate,

00:02:55.277 --> 00:02:58.302
resourced pathway at tier five.

00:02:58.392 --> 00:03:01.029
For cities, from baseline monitoring to
an immediate, system-level response.

00:03:01.321 --> 00:03:03.002
Which lever matters most?

00:03:03.144 --> 00:03:05.454
Move each factor by ten points in the

00:03:05.454 --> 00:03:08.991
risk-reducing direction
and see how far R falls.

00:03:09.206 --> 00:03:14.014
In this example likelihood
moves it most, by 0.029,

00:03:14.205 --> 00:03:17.586
followed by vulnerability,
exposure and resilience.

00:03:17.858 --> 00:03:20.742
But equal steps are not equal efforts,

00:03:20.907 --> 00:03:23.954
and likelihood is often
set by nature or biology,

00:03:24.179 --> 00:03:28.630
so programmes mostly act through
exposure, vulnerability and resilience.

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:32.607
R measures residual risk,
not how bad a miss would be.

00:03:32.873 --> 00:03:35.532
A second layer, expected annual loss,

00:03:35.706 --> 00:03:39.701
multiplies likelihood by
consequence and by one minus rho.

00:03:39.946 --> 00:03:42.385
Take two liver patients in the same tier.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:48.224
Patient A has R of 0.072 and
would be caught early and cured.

00:03:48.557 --> 00:03:53.687
Patient B has R of 0.066
and would present advanced.

00:03:53.877 --> 00:03:55.650
R ranks A first,

00:03:55.804 --> 00:04:02.468
but EAL is 0.20 for B against 0.072 for A,

00:04:02.682 --> 00:04:05.585
nearly three times higher. Within a tier,

00:04:05.675 --> 00:04:10.228
EAL shows who has the most to
lose if surveillance is slow.

00:04:10.505 --> 00:04:16.722
One engine, two domains. The same three
indicator families feed E, V and rho.

00:04:17.063 --> 00:04:21.982
In disaster science, hazards, climate
change and urbanisation raise exposure;

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:27.550
poverty, governance and pollution raise
vulnerability; and risk-management strategy,

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.304
infrastructure, and health and
water security supply resilience.

00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:35.857
In liver cancer, the size of the
at-risk population feeds exposure;

00:04:36.104 --> 00:04:37.451
reduced liver reserve,

00:04:37.451 --> 00:04:41.146
metabolic disease and fibrosis
progression feed vulnerability;

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:43.915
and surveillance uptake, test quality,

00:04:43.915 --> 00:04:47.458
access to cure and care
coordination feed resilience.

00:04:47.702 --> 00:04:51.602
That is RMTI: one bounded equation, four
levers, five fixed tiers, and two domains.

00:04:51.826 --> 00:04:54.608
Next, see it applied:
liver cancer surveillance,

00:04:54.608 --> 00:04:56.976
and flood and heat risk in African cities.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.851
Thank you. I’m Dr. Cesar Marolla.

00:05:03.048 --> 00:05:07.931
The code is open under Apache 2.0,
the demo uses no patient data,

00:05:08.103 --> 00:05:11.120
and nothing here is medical
or engineering advice.

